ool_run_002/summary.json\n{\n  \"errors\": {\n    \"select_data|sample_sheet\": \"Parameter 'sample_sheet': invalid dataset id 'f9cad7b01a472135f6700804526d44a7'\"\n  },\n  \"evidence_directory\": \"run_trace/galaxy_tool_run_002\",\n  \"status\": \"validation_failed\",\n  \"submitted\": false,\n  \"tool_id\": \"toolshed.g2.bx.psu.edu/repos/iuc/deseq2/deseq2/2.11.40.8+galaxy4\"\n}\n\nFILE /workspace/run_trace/galaxy_tool_run_002/validation_response.json\n{\n  \"action\": \"/tool_runner/index\",\n  \"citations\": true,\n  \"creator\": null,\n  \"credentials\": [],\n  \"description\": \"Determines differentially expressed features from count tables\",\n  \"display\": true,\n  \"edam_operations\": [\n    \"operation_3800\"\n  ],\n  \"edam_topics\": [\n    \"topic_3308\"\n  ],\n  \"enctype\": \"application/x-www-form-urlencoded\",\n  \"errors\": {\n    \"select_data|sample_sheet\": \"Parameter 'sample_sheet': invalid dataset id 'f9cad7b01a472135f6700804526d44a7'\"\n  },\n  \"form_style\": \"regular\",\n  \"has_parameters\": true,\n  \"help\": \"<p class=\\\"infomark\\\"><strong>What it does</strong></p>\\n<p>Uses DESeq2 version 1.40.2 to estimate variance-mean dependence in count data from high-throughput sequencing assays and test for differential expression based on a model using the negative binomial distribution.</p>\\n<hr class=\\\"docutils\\\" />\\n<p><strong>Inputs</strong></p>\\n<p><strong>Count Files</strong></p>\\n<p><a class=\\\"reference external\\\" href=\\\"http://master.bioconductor.org/packages/release/bioc/html/DESeq2.html\\\">DESeq2</a> takes count tables generated from <strong>featureCounts</strong>, <strong>HTSeq-count</strong> or <strong>StringTie</strong> as input. Count tables must be generated for each sample individually. One header row is assumed, but files with no header (e.g from HTSeq) can be input with the <em>Files have header?</em> option set to No. DESeq2 is capable of handling multiple factors that affect your experiment. The first factor you input is considered as the primary factor that affects gene expressions. Optionally, you can input one or more secondary factors that might influence your experiment. But the final output will be changes in genes due to primary factor in presence of secondary factors. Each factor has two levels/states. You need to select appropriate count table from your history for each factor level.</p>\\n<p>The following table gives some examples of factors and their levels:</p>\\n<table border=\\\"1\\\" class=\\\"docutils\\\">\\n<colgroup>\\n<col width=\\\"24%\\\" />\\n<col width=\\\"37%\\\" />\\n<col width=\\\"39%\\\" />\\n</colgroup>\\n<tbody valign=\\\"top\\\">\\n<tr><td>Factor</td>\\n<td>Factor level 1</td>\\n<td>Factor level 2</td>\\n</tr>\\n<tr><td>Treatment</td>\\n<td>Treated</td>\\n<td>Untreated</td>\\n</tr>\\n<tr><td>Condition</td>\\n<td>Knockdown</td>\\n<td>Wildtype</td>\\n</tr>\\n<tr><td>TimePoint</td>\\n<td>Day4</td>\\n<td>Day1</td>\\n</tr>\\n<tr><td>SeqType</td>\\n<td>SingleEnd</td>\\n<td>PairedEnd</td>\\n</tr>\\n<tr><td>Gender</td>\\n<td>Female</td>\\n<td>Male</td>\\n</tr>\\n</tbody>\\n</table>\\n<p><em>Note</em>: Output log2 fold changes are based on primary factor level 1 vs. factor level2. Here the order of factor levels is important. For example, for the factor 'Treatment' given in above table, DESeq2 computes fold changes of 'Treated' samples against 'Untreated', i.e. the values correspond to up or down regulations of genes in Treated samples.</p>\\n<p><a class=\\\"reference external\\\" href=\\\"http://master.bioconductor.org/packages/release/bioc/html/DESeq2.html\\\">DESeq2</a> can also take transcript-level counts from quantification tools such as, <strong>kallisto</strong>, <strong>Salmon</strong> and <strong>Sailfish</strong>, and this Galaxy wrapper incorporates the Bioconductor <a class=\\\"reference external\\\" href=\\\"https://bioconductor.org/packages/devel/bioc/vignettes/tximport/inst/doc/tximport.html\\\">tximport</a> package to process the transcript counts for DESeq2.</p>\\n<p><strong>Salmon or Sailfish Files</strong></p>\\n<p>Salmon or Sailfish <tt class=\\\"docutils literal\\\">quant.sf</tt> files can be imported by setting type to <em>Salmon</em> or <em>Sailfish</em> respectively above. Note: for previous version of Salmon or Sailfish, in which the quant.sf files start with comment lines you will need to remove the comment lines before inputting here. An example of the format is shown below.</p>\\n<p>Example:</p>\\n<table border=\\\"1\\\" class=\\\"docutils\\\">\\n<colgroup>\\n<col width=\\\"20%\\\" />\\n<col width=\\\"17%\\\" />\\n<col width=\\\"25%\\\" />\\n<col width=\\\"1\n--- /workspace/run_trace/galaxy_tool_run_003\nrequest.json\nparameter_provenance.json\nsummary.json\nsubmission_response.json\nvalidation_response.json\nvalidation_parameter_provenance.json\nFILE /workspace/run_trace/galaxy_tool_run_003/request.json\n{\n  \"history_id\": \"bbd44e69cb8906b56bbc515c55c48d53\",\n  \"input_format\": \"21.01\",\n  \"selected_input_format\": \"21.01\",\n  \"tool_id\": \"toolshed.g2.bx.psu.edu/repos/iuc/deseq2/deseq2/2.11.40.8+galaxy4\",\n  \"tool_inputs\": {\n    \"advanced_options|auto_mean_filter_off\": false,\n    \"advanced_options|esf_cond|esf\": \"\",\n    \"advanced_options|fit_type\": \"1\",\n    \"advanced_options|lfc_shrinkage_type\": \"apeglm\",\n    \"advanced_options|outlier_filter_off\": false,\n    \"advanced_options|outlier_replace_off\": false,\n    \"advanced_options|prefilter_conditional|prefilter\": \"\",\n    \"advanced_options|use_beta_priors\": false,\n    \"header\": true,\n    \"output_options|alpha_ma\": 0.1,\n    \"output_options|output_selector\": \"sizefactors\",\n    \"select_data|countsFile\": {\n      \"id\": \"5f42de937948a619\",\n      \"src\": \"hdca\"\n    },\n    \"select_data|design_formula_mode|design_formula\": \"~ sex + condition\",\n    \"select_data|design_formula_mode|mode\": \"custom\",\n    \"select_data|design_formula_mode|reference_level\": \"Control